"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a Gleason 4+3, ISUP grade group 3, and pT2a stage, with positive surgical margins. Seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymph node metastasis was absent. The biopsy demonstrated a Gleason 4+3, ISUP grade group 2. These differences between biopsy and prostatectomy findings introduce uncertainty regarding the true extent of the disease. The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding. The positive surgical margins represent a factor that may influence prognosis. The preoperative PSA was 7.8. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their trend is crucial for refining the prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence. The fixed estimated horizon is 49.38 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.03–61.72 months; its coverage has not been validated."
